WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Deep learning has been successfully applied to computer vision,

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.720
national language processing, speech recognition, online advertising, logistics,

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.680
many many many problems. There are a few things that are unique about the application of deep

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.560
learning to computer vision, about the status of computer vision. In this video, I'm going to

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.000
share with you some of my observations about deep learning for computer vision in the hope that

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.480
that will help you better navigate the literature and the set of ideas out there and how you build

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:36.400
these systems yourself for computer vision. So you can think of most machine learning problems as

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:43.040
falling somewhere on the spectrum between where you have relatively little data to where you have

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:52.400
lots of data. So for example, I think that today we have a decent amount of data for speech

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.880
recognition, at least relative to the complexity of the problem. And even though there are

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.320
reasonably large data sets today for image recognition or image classification,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.240
because image recognition is just a complicated problem to look at all those pixels and figure

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.640
out what it is, it feels like even though the online data sets are quite big, you know,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:21.600
like over a million images, feels like we still wish we had more data. And there are some

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:29.600
problems like object detection, where we have even less data. So just as a reminder, image

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.040
recognition was the problem of looking at the picture and telling you, is this a cat or not?

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.640
Whereas object detection is looking at the picture and actually putting the bounding boxes

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.800
and telling you where in the picture of the objects, which are the calls are as well.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.920
And so because of the cost of getting the bounding boxes, it's just more expensive to

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.280
label the objects and the bounding boxes. So we tend to have less data for object detection,

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.280
than for image recognition. And object detection is something we'll discuss next week.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.400
So if you look across a broad spectrum of machine learning problems, you see on average that

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:15.760
when you have a lot of data, you tend to find people getting away with using simpler algorithms,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.480
as well as less hand engineering. So there's just less needing to carefully design features

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.280
for the problem. But instead, you can have a giant neural network, even a simpler architecture,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.680
and have a neural network just learn whatever it wants to learn, when you have a lot of data.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.720
Whereas in contrast, when you don't have damaged data, then on average, you see people engaging

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:45.520
in more hand engineering. And if you want to be ungenerous, you know, you can say there are more

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:53.520
hacks. But I think when you don't have much data, then hand engineering is actually the best way

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:01.760
to get good performance. So when I look at machine learning applications, I think, usually we have

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.160
the learning algorithm has two sources of knowledge. One source of knowledge is the

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.560
labeled data, really the x y pairs you use for supervised learning. And the second source of

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:17.440
knowledge is the hand engineering. And there are lots of ways to hand engineer a system, it can be

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.680
from carefully hand designing the features to carefully hand designing the network architectures,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.640
to maybe other components of your system. And so when you don't have much labeled data,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:36.000
you just have to count more on hand engineering. And so I think computer vision is trying to learn

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.800
a really complex function. And it often feels like we don't have enough data for computer vision,

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.600
even though data sets are getting bigger and bigger. Often we just don't have as much data

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:53.680
as we need. And this is why the state of computer vision, historically, and even today, has relied

00:03:53.680 --> 00:04:00.640
more on hand engineering. And I think this is also why the field of computer vision has

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:08.720
developed rather complex network architectures, is because in the absence of more data,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.800
the way to get good performance is to spend more time architecting or, you know, fooling around

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:21.200
with the network architecture. And in case you think I'm being derogatory of hand engineering,

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.680
that's not at all my intent. When you don't have enough data, hand engineering is a very difficult,

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:35.200
very skillful task that requires a lot of insight. And someone that is insightful in hand engineering

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.080
will get better performance. And it's a great contribution to a project to do that hand

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.840
engineering when you don't have enough data. It's just when you have lots of data, then I wouldn't

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.720
spend time in hand engineering, I would spend time, you know, building up the learning system

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:57.440
instead. But I think historically, the field of computer vision has used very small data sets

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.800
and so historically, the computer vision literature has relied on a lot of hand engineering.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.680
And even though in the last few years, the amount of data we throw at computer vision

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:14.320
task has increased dramatically. I think that that has resulted in a significant reduction

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.120
in the amount of hand engineering that's being done. But there's still a lot of hand engineering

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.080
of network architectures and computer vision, which is why you see very complicated

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:29.920
hyper parameter choices in computer vision are more complex than you do in a lot of other disciplines.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:37.120
And in fact, because you usually have smaller object detection data sets than image recognition

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:44.000
data sets, when we talk about object detection, that is tasks like this next week, you see that

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:50.000
the algorithms or if you want to be, you see that the algorithms become even more complex and

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:57.520
has even more, you know, specialized components. Fortunately, one thing that helps a lot when you

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:08.880
have little data is transfer learning. And I would say, for the example from the previous slide of the

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:17.440
Tigger-Misty-Nether detection problem, you have so little data that transfer learning, you know,

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.640
will help a lot. And so that's another set of techniques that's used a lot for when you have

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:28.000
relatively little data. If you look at the computer vision literature, look at the set of

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:34.480
ideas out there, you also find that people are really enthusiastic, they're really into doing well

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:40.080
on standardized benchmark data sets, and on winning competitions. And for computer vision

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.600
researchers, if you do well on a benchmark, it's easier to get the paper published. So there's

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.600
just a lot of attention on doing well on these benchmarks. And the positive side of this is that

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:57.120
it helps the whole community figure out what are the most effective algorithms. But you also see

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:03.360
in the papers, people do things that allow you to do well on a benchmark, but that you wouldn't

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.280
really use in a production or a system that you deploy in an actual application. So here are a few

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:15.840
tips for doing well on benchmarks. These are things that I don't myself pretty much ever use if I'm

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:22.560
putting a system into production, that is to actually serve customers. But one is ensemble.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.520
And what that means is, after you've figured out what neural network you want,

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:34.720
train several neural networks independently and average their outputs. So initialize, say,

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:40.080
three or five or seven neural networks randomly, and train up all of these neural networks,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.640
and then average their outputs. And by the way, it's important to average their outputs,

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:50.080
my hat, don't average their weights, that won't work. Look at your, say, seven neural networks,

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:55.280
they'll have seven different predictions and average that. And this will cause you to do

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:02.880
maybe 1% better or 2% better or so a little bit better on some benchmark. And this will cause

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.680
you to do a little bit better, you know, maybe sometimes as much as 1% or 2%, which can really

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:15.600
help win a competition. But because ensembling means that to test on each image, you might need

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:21.520
to run an image through anywhere from, say, three to 15 different networks, which is quite typical.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.280
This slows down your running time by a factor of three to 15 or sometimes even more. And so

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:31.840
ensembling is one of those tips that people use for doing well on benchmarks and for winning

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:38.240
competitions. But that I think is almost never used in production to serve actual customers,

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.120
I guess, unless you have a huge computational budget and don't mind burning a lot more of it

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:50.720
per customer image. Another thing you see in papers that really helps on benchmarks is

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:58.160
multi crop and test time. So what I mean by that is you've seen how you can do data augmentation,

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:04.240
and multi crop is a form of applying data augmentation to your test image as well.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:10.960
So for example, let's see if a cat image is copied four times, including two mirrored versions.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:18.000
There's a technique called the 10 crop, which basically says, let's say you take this central

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:24.000
region, that crop and run it through your classifier, and then take that crop up the left hand

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.880
corner run through your classifier up right hand corner, shown in green, lower left, shown in yellow,

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:35.920
lower right, shown in orange, and run that through your classifier. And then do the same thing with

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:42.320
the mirrored image, right? So take the central crop, then take the four corners crops. So that's

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:46.960
one central crop here and here, there's four corners crop here and here. And if you add these

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:52.720
up, there's 10 different crops of the image. So hence the name 10 crop. And so what you do is

00:09:52.800 --> 00:10:00.450
you run these 10 images through your classifier and then average the results. So if you have the

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:05.170
computational budget, you could do this, maybe don't need as many as 10 crops, you can use a few

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:11.170
crops. And this might get you a little bit better performance in a production system. By

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:18.050
production, I mean, a system you're deploying for actual users. But this is another technique

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:22.850
that is used much more for doing well on benchmarks than in actual production systems.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:30.130
And one of the big problems of ensembling is that you need to keep all these different networks

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:35.490
around. And so that just takes up a lot more computer memory. For multi-crop, I guess at

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:42.130
least you keep just one network around so it doesn't suck up as much memory, but it still

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:50.530
slows down your runtime quite a bit. So these are tips you see, and research papers will refer

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:57.410
to these tips as well. But I personally do not tend to use these methods when building production

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:02.130
systems, even though they are great for doing better on benchmarks and on winning competitions.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:09.890
Because a lot of computer vision problems are in a small data regime, others have done a lot of hand

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:15.170
engineering of the network architectures. And in your network that works well on one vision

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:20.210
problem, often maybe surprisingly, but it just often will work on other vision problems as well.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:28.290
So to build a practical system, often you do well starting off with someone else's new network

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:33.570
architecture. And you can use an open source implementation if possible, because the open

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:38.930
source implementation might have figured out all the finicky details like the learning rate

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:45.010
decay schedule and other hyper parameters. And finally, someone else might have spent

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:53.650
weeks training a model on half a dozen GPUs and on over a million images. And so by using someone

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:58.690
else's pre-trained model and fine tuning on your data set, you can often get going much faster on

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:04.450
an application. But of course, if you have the compute resources and the inclination,

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:08.530
don't let me stop you from training your own networks from scratch.

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:15.330
And in fact, if you want to invent your own computer vision algorithm, that's what you might have to do.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:21.650
So that's it for this week. I hope that seeing a number of computer vision architectures helps

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:27.090
you get a sense of what works. In this week's programming exercises, you actually learn

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:32.770
another programming framework and use that to implement ResNet. So I hope you enjoyed

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:36.930
that programming exercise, and I look forward to seeing you next week.
